


“You’re going to put those next to each other,” he said.
“Yes.”
“So I look wrong before anything happens.”
Maren carried the main camera beneath the shelter roof and opened the tripod. “The plaque was here before we heard the cry.”
“And you’ll put it before my take.”
“Yes.”
He set the audio case on the picnic table. “Then keep the part where you don’t know what was here before the shelter.”
“I kept your full take.”
“That isn’t the same thing.”
Maren locked the tripod head, then shifted the frame until the fireplace and the plaque both remained visible. “Then it stays next to the correction.”
Jonah looked at the monitor, then opened the case.




They unpacked in practiced order. Maren positioned the fixed camera outside the shelter where it could cover both the entrance and the path. She placed the audio recorder at the center of the picnic table and ran a cable to two boundary microphones clipped beneath the roof beam. Jonah assembled the battery lights and aimed them at the fireplace without switching them on.




The generator remained outside the rear wall with its fuel valve closed. Maren filmed its position, the cable path, and the phones resting together on the table. She noted the nearest road noise, the river, the tapping branch, insects, and their own footfalls on concrete and gravel. Jonah walked each open side while she listened through headphones.




His steps changed at the rear wall. The stone returned a short, hard echo. At the open sides, the sound spread into the clearing and lost its edge.




“Again,” she said.




He repeated the circuit.




Maren marked the difference on the route sheet. She then recorded one minute with both of them seated and every powered device off. The headphones carried water, insects, the branch, a distant engine, and Jonah’s breathing when he leaned too close to the table microphone.




She held up one finger when he started to speak.




The minute ended.




“Baseline one,” she said. “Shelter. All production equipment off except recorders. No generator. No lights. No emitter.”




Jonah looked at her.




Maren removed the headphones. “Take it out.”




He opened the side compartment of the audio case. The black box lay beneath a coil of spare cable. The strip of tape across its casing still read H.R. TEST.




“You knew it was there,” he said.




Maren held out her hand.




Jonah looked at it.




She did not lower her hand.




He set it on the table. “Then tell me to put it back.”




Maren looked at the plaque beyond the shelter, the official map beneath her hand, and the camera framing Jonah with the emitter between them. They had corrected the building. They had recorded the route. They had one clean minute of room tone and two passes of his footsteps. She could stop the device, repack the case, and keep every condition they had signed.




Jonah rested his uninjured hand on the toggle switch.




“You said the sound was the reason we came,” he said. “This was running before we heard it.”




“Then it contaminates the test.”




“It was also part of the setup last time.”




Maren checked the time. The sun had dropped below the trees. Their fixed camera had enough light for another few minutes before the image shifted to infrared. She could have taken the emitter from him in the office. At the gate, while Ruth and Lena were still there, she could have opened the side compartment and made him leave it in the car. She had done neither. Now she could make him put the box away, or keep him at the table and record the activation, the distance, and every question that followed.




“Generator first,” she said. “We log both devices. Nothing counts as a baseline after they come on.”




Jonah carried the power cable through the rear opening. Maren followed with the camera and filmed him open the fuel valve, pull the starter cord, and step back.




The engine caught on the second pull.




A low mechanical hum entered the clearing. It reached the shelter through the rear wall and returned from the fireplace in a flatter, heavier note. In the headphones, the generator masked most of the river noise.




Jonah connected the emitter, placed it on the picnic table, and looked at Maren.




She kept the lens on his hand.




He raised the toggle.




A thin electronic whine joined the generator. The fixed camera’s image flickered once as its exposure shifted. The branch continued tapping the roof, but Maren had to remove one side of the headphones to hear it.




Their clean baseline had lasted sixty seconds.




Scene 4 — The First Cry




The electronic whine was sharper through Maren’s headphones than it was in the shelter. It sat above the generator’s low vibration and below the insect noise outside, a narrow tone that became harder to ignore each time she listened for something behind it.




She filmed the emitter on the picnic table. “Emitter active at seven forty-three and twelve seconds. Generator active. Main camera, fixed camera, and three audio recorders running.”




Jonah sat across from her with both hands visible. The bandage on his right hand had loosened near the wrist. He pressed the tape down with his thumb.




“Can I start?”




“Wait.”




Maren turned toward each open side of the shelter and held the camera steady for five seconds. The clearing had lost its remaining color. The fixed camera’s infrared lamps showed as faint red points beside the trail. Beyond them, her eyes could separate only the first line of trunks from the dark between them.




She checked the audio recorder. The left channel showed the level from the shelter microphones. The right showed the level from the recorder near the trail. Both meters trembled continuously from the generator.




“All right,” she said. “One question. Then thirty seconds.”




Jonah looked toward the fireplace instead of the camera. “Is anyone here with us?”




Maren started the timer on her watch.




The generator continued at the same pitch. The emitter whined. Insects pulsed outside the shelter, several overlapping rhythms that weakened and returned without stopping together. A branch tapped the roof twice. Jonah shifted one foot beneath the table.




At thirty seconds, Maren said, “No distinct response.”




“You don’t have to say that after every question.”




“I do if we’re going to remember the quiet parts.”




He rubbed the edge of the bandage with his left thumbnail. “Did you make the sound we heard here before?”




She started the timer again.




Maren heard no sound she could separate from the equipment and the clearing. Halfway through the interval, a vehicle passed on the distant road. Its tires rose into a soft wash, held for several seconds, and faded.




“Road traffic,” Maren said.




Jonah watched the darkness beyond the fireplace. “You didn’t see the truck.”




“No.”




“I heard the tires too.”




“Then put that in your notes.”




He reached for the route sheet.




Maren kept the camera on the open side behind him.




He turned the sheet over and wrote with his left hand.




His next question came before the full interval had ended.




“Did you send me away?”




Maren lowered the camera. “Start again.”




“I waited.”




“Not thirty seconds.”




“Twenty-five.”




“Start again.”




Jonah leaned back. The picnic table creaked beneath his weight. “You’re not going to let anything build.”
